  <data name="ai_concept_atomic_number" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The **Atomic Number** is the number of protons in an atom. It's the primary way elements are ordered in the periodic table.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ai_concept_electron" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>**Electrons** are negatively charged particles that orbit the nucleus. They are involved in bonding and electricity.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ai_concept_electronegativity" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>**Electronegativity** measures how strongly an atom pulls electrons towards itself in a chemical bond. Fluorine is the most electronegative!</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ai_concept_generic" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>That's a core concept in chemistry! Which part of it would you like me to dive deeper into? I know about protons, bonding, radioactivity, and more.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ai_concept_group" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A **Group** is a vertical column. Elements in a group usually have similar chemical properties and the same number of valence electrons.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ai_concept_ionization" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>**Ionization energy** is the energy required to remove an electron from an atom. It generally increases as you move right across the periodic table.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ai_concept_isotope" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>An **isotope** is a variation of an element with the same number of protons but a different number of neutrons. This affects its atomic mass!</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ai_concept_neutron" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>**Neutrons** are neutral particles in the nucleus. They add mass and help stabilize the nucleus, especially in heavier elements.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ai_concept_orbital" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>**Orbitals** are regions in space around a nucleus where there is a high probability of finding an electron. They have shapes like s, p, d, and f.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ai_concept_period" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A **Period** is a horizontal row in the periodic table. Elements in the same period have the same number of electron shells.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ai_concept_proton" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>**Protons** are positively charged particles in the nucleus. The number of protons (Atomic Number) defines which element it is!</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ai_concept_radioactivity" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>**Radioactivity** is the process by which an unstable atomic nucleus loses energy by emitting radiation. Elements like Uranium are famous for this!</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ai_concept_shell" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Electrons reside in **shells** (energy levels) around the nucleus. The configuration determines the element's position in the table.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ai_concept_valence" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>**Valence electrons** are the electrons in the outermost shell of an atom. they determine how an element reacts with others.</value>
  </data>
